BT /F1 16 Tf 50 740 Td (Chapter 2: Chunking Strategies and Dense Embeddings) Tj ET
50 725 m 550 725 l S
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 700 Td (2.1 Document Preprocessing and Normalization) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 684 Td (Raw documents such as PDFs often contain complex layouts, headers, footers, and hyphenated) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 668 Td (line breaks. Normalizing whitespace and segmenting documents into logical sections) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 652 Td (is vital before chunking.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 624 Td (2.2 Semantic Chunking vs Sliding Window) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 608 Td (Fixed sliding window chunking uses a defined character or token size \(e.g. 500-800 characters\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 592 Td (with an overlap \(e.g. 100 characters\) to preserve contextual boundaries across split points.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 576 Td (Semantic chunking respects paragraph and section headings to maintain coherent ideas.) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 548 Td (2.3 Sentence Transformers and Dense Vectors) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 532 Td (Sentence Transformers map variable-length textual chunks into dense vector representations) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 516 Td (\(typically 384 or 768 dimensions\). The all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model offers a superior balance of) Tj ET
BT /F2 11 Tf 50 500 Td (computational efficiency, low latency, and high retrieval accuracy on semantic similarity benchmarks.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9 Tf 270 30 Td (Page 1 of 5 - RAG Architecture Guide) Tj ET
endstream
